# Vite drops dot-directories from public/, so the Android app-links file is
# kept at well-known/ and served at the path Android looks for. nginx does the
# same with an alias; this is the Pages spelling of it. 200 = rewrite, not a
# visible redirect. The SPA fallback (any unknown path → index.html) is what
# Pages does by default for a single-page app and needs no rule here.
/.well-known/assetlinks.json  /well-known/assetlinks.json  200
/.well-known/apple-app-site-association  /well-known/apple-app-site-association  200

# The public legal pages (privacy.html, terms.html, delete-account.html) are
# served at /privacy, /terms and /delete-account by Pages' own extensionless
# handling -- it serves foo.html at /foo and 308s /foo.html to /foo. A rewrite
# rule here mapping /privacy back to /privacy.html made a redirect loop, so
# there is deliberately no rule for them.

# THE SPA FALLBACK, WRITTEN DOWN (2026-10-13: every deep link answered 404
# with an empty body -- /institution_admin/students/student_360, and every
# other screen, so a refresh or a pasted link showed nothing at all; only
# /login worked, because the Function owns that path).
#
# The note above said Pages falls back to index.html for a single-page app on
# its own and needs no rule. It does not, or does not reliably once
# _routes.json is restricting Functions to a path list, which is the shape
# this project has. The rule is cheap, it is the documented spelling, and it
# cannot be lost to a default changing under us.
#
# It is the LAST rule on purpose: a real file still wins, the rewrites above
# still win, and /api/* never reaches here because _routes.json sends it to
# the Function first. 200 = rewrite, so the URL the person typed survives for
# the router to read.
/*  /index.html  200
